<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Two project references, and neither is Boltway.AuthorizationServer. That absence is the
    point being demonstrated: these two halves deploy apart, and it is a fact about the dependency
    graph rather than a claim in a document.

    This list said "and nothing else" while it had one entry. It has two now, because a resource
    server that wants to survive a key rotation has to fetch a JWKS, and the one assembly in this
    solution permitted to make an outbound request is Boltway.OAuth.Net - a structural rule with
    an architecture test behind it (Only_the_guarded_fetcher_touches_system_net_http). So the honest
    version of the claim is the one now written above: not "one reference", but "not the
    authorization server". The count was never what mattered.

    Boltway.OAuth.Net is deliberately not a dependency of the Boltway.ResourceServer
    package itself. A deployment that supplies its keys by hand - an authorization server hosting its
    own protected resource, pointing at its own key ring - should not acquire an HTTP transport it
    never calls. Taking the refresher is the host's decision, so it is the host's reference.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Boltway.ResourceServer\Boltway.ResourceServer.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\Boltway.OAuth.Net\Boltway.OAuth.Net.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
